topics.referenceMarkdown:
**Definition:** Metadata API â€” `export const metadata` / `generateMetadata` â€” plus Open Graph/Twitter cards, JSON-LD structured data, `sitemap.ts`, `robots.ts`, and canonical URLs.

```tsx
// app/products/[id]/page.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next';

export async function generateMetadata({ params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const { id } = await params;
  const product = await getProduct(id);          // dedupe: may share a cache() with the page
  if (!product) return {};
  return {
    title: { default: 'Acme Store', template: '%s Â· Acme Store' },
    description: product.summary,
    alternates: { canonical: `/products/${product.slug}` },
    openGraph: {
      type: 'website',
      title: product.name,
      description: product.summary,
      url: `/products/${product.slug}`,
      images: [{ url: product.ogImage, width: 1200, height: 630, alt: product.name }],
    },
    twitter: { card: 'summary_large_image', title: product.name, images: [product.ogImage] },
    robots: product.discontinued ? { index: false, follow: true } : undefined,
  };
}
```
```ts
// app/sitemap.ts â€” returns URLs; revalidate with export const revalidate = 3600
import type { MetadataRoute } from 'next';
export default async function sitemap(): Promise<MetadataRoute.Sitemap> {
  const products = await db.product.findMany({ select: { slug: true, updatedAt: true } });
  return [
    { url: 'https://acme.example', lastModified: new Date(), changeFrequency: 'daily', priority: 1 },
    ...products.map(p => ({ url: `https://acme.example/products/${p.slug}`, lastModified: p.updatedAt })),
  ];
}
```
```ts
// app/robots.ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from 'next';
export default function robots(): MetadataRoute.Robots {
  return {
    rules: [{ userAgent: '*', allow: '/', disallow: ['/api/', '/admin/'] }],
    sitemap: 'https://acme.example/sitemap.xml',
    host: 'https://acme.example',
  };
}
```
```tsx
// JSON-LD: a <script type="application/ld+json"> rendered by a Server Component.
// Values must be JSON-escaped: JSON.stringify handles </script>; never interpolate raw text.
export function ProductJsonLd({ p }: { p: Product }) {
  const data = {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Product',
    name: p.name,
    image: [p.imageUrl],
    description: p.summary,
    sku: p.sku,
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: (p.priceCents / 100).toFixed(2), priceCurrency: 'USD', availability: 'https://schema.org/InStock' },
    aggregateRating: p.rating ? { '@type': 'AggregateRating', ratingValue: p.rating, reviewCount: p.reviewCount } : undefined,
  };
  return <script type="application/ld+json" dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: JSON.stringify(data) }} />;
}
```

**Why CSR-only content is a problem for crawlers.** Some crawlers execute JavaScript; many social scrapers and preview bots do not. Content that only exists after hydration is invisible to them: link previews are blank, and content discovery depends on rendering budget. Even for JS-capable crawlers, client-rendered content is later and less reliably indexed, and hydration failures turn your page into an empty shell for that crawler. Therefore: server-render anything indexable, keep metadata in the head server-side (streaming metadata does this progressively), and give crawlers real URLs via `sitemap.ts`. Also handle `hreflang` for locales and canonical URLs to prevent duplicate-content dilution (canonical entry: PHASE 7 â†’ Networking â†’ CORS & caching; canonical entry: PHASE 6 â†’ Indexing for the database-side analogue).

**Questions**
1. How do you set page metadata dynamically?
2. What are Open Graph and Twitter cards for?
3. How do you generate a sitemap without hand-writing it?
4. Why does CSR-only content hurt SEO?
5. What is JSON-LD and what is it used for?
6. When do you need `robots.ts` vs a static file?
7. What is a canonical URL for, and what goes wrong without it?
8. How do you handle per-locale metadata?
9. What is streaming metadata?
10. Where should SEO-critical content live?

**Model answer, Q4 (senior/staff framing):** *Context.* A marketing page renders its content only after a client-side fetch; traffic is fine but organic traffic is flat and link previews are blank. *Decision.* Server-render or prerender anything indexable, and treat client fetching as an enhancement. *Reasoning.* Crawlers vary in whether and how well they execute JavaScript; social scrapers generally do not execute it at all; and even JS-capable crawlers allocate limited rendering budget and penalize failures or errors during hydration. Content that depends on hydration is therefore discovered later, indexed less reliably, and never seen at all by non-executing scrapers. The specific failure here â€” blank link previews â€” is a direct consequence: the OG tags that would produce the preview are either absent from the initial HTML or the preview scraper sees an empty body. *Trade-offs.* Server-rendering the content costs TTFB and server compute versus a static shell; prerendering (SSG/ISR) gets most of the SEO benefit at near-zero cost but requires build-time or revalidation-time data access, which means a build can fail on stale or unavailable data and dynamic segments need `generateStaticParams`. *Failure scenario.* A redesign converts every page to CSR with `useEffect` fetching; rankings drop 30% over two months, and the diagnosis is confusing because "it works in the browser" and Core Web Vitals in the field are fine. *Prevention.* Put indexable content in the server/prerendered path, assert in CI that key pages contain the expected text in the raw HTML response, keep `generateMetadata` server-side, and monitor crawler logs for 5xx/JS errors.

**Mistakes:** relying on `<title>` in a client component; forgetting canonical URLs with query-param variants; `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with interpolated user text in JSON-LD (XSS); indexing `?page=2` duplicates; assuming crawlers render.

**Mini task:** build `/products/[slug]` with `generateMetadata` (title template, canonical, OG image, `robots: noindex` for discontinued items), JSON-LD `Product` with `offers`, `sitemap.ts` generated from the DB, and `robots.ts` disallowing `/admin`. Then `curl` the HTML and confirm every tag is in the *initial* response, not added by JS. Finally, run a Google rich-results-style validator or a structured-data linter and fix what it flags.

