topics.referenceMarkdown:
âš« Expert Â· **Must-know** Â· Prerequisites: 4.5.3 (nesting), 4.5.7 (focus), 4.2.1 (App Router conventions)

**Definition:** **Parallel routes** render multiple independent route slots into the *same layout at the same time* â€” so one layout can host, say, a feed and an authenticated sidebar that load and refresh independently. **Intercepting routes** render one slot *in place of* its normal position when the navigation came from a specific origin (a `<Link>` from the feed, a form submission) while falling back to the normal position on a hard navigation (a direct URL load, a refresh, a paste). Together they give you the "modal routing" pattern that makes a photo lightbox feel instant while keeping the URL shareable and the deep link correct.

**Why it exists â€” the problem it solves.** A photo lightbox done naively has two bad options: (a) render a modal without changing the URL â€” fast and pretty, but not shareable and Back closes the whole app; (b) navigate to `/photos/42` and render a full page â€” shareable, but the feed is destroyed and you lose the instant feel. Intercepting routes give you *both*: the URL becomes `/photos/42` (shareable, Back works, SSR/hard-load correct) **and** the transition from the feed is a local render with the feed still mounted behind the modal.

**Mechanics in Next.js App Router** (the canonical implementation):

```
app/
  layout.tsx                 // the ROOT layout: html/body
  @feed/                     // a PARALLEL SLOT â€” rendered into <Feed> in layout.tsx
    default.tsx              // fallback for the slot when it can't be rendered on this navigation (REQUIRED)
    page.tsx
  @modal/                    // a second parallel slot
    default.tsx
    (.)photos/[id]/page.tsx  // (.) = intercept from the SAME segment level
    photos/[id]/page.tsx     // hmm â€” the real pair is usually:
  photos/[id]/page.tsx       // the real route: renders FULL PAGE on a hard load / refresh
  photos/page.tsx            // the feed (or gallery) route

// (.)  same level        (..)  one level above        (...)  from the root app directory
// Matching rules for an intercepted route:
//  Â· Soft navigation from the matching origin  -> render the INTERCEPTED route (inside the slot)
//  Â· Hard navigation (first load, refresh, paste, external link) -> render the REAL route full page
```

```tsx
// Root layout declares the slots. Layouts RECEIVE slot props; they do not render slot components themselves.
export default function RootLayout({ children, feed, modal }: {
  children: React.ReactNode;      // the implicit `children` slot (the main content)
  feed: React.ReactNode;          // @feed
  modal: React.ReactNode;         // @modal
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        {feed}                     // independent slot: its own loading/error boundary, refetches on its own
        {children}
        {modal}                    // the intercepted modal renders here on soft navigation
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// The modal route itself: it must behave differently on a hard load (render a real page) vs a soft one
// (render a dialog over the feed). You detect that with the router or by checking the navigation type.
'use client';
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';
import { Dialog } from './Dialog';

export default function PhotoModal({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const router = useRouter();
  const isSoftNavigation = typeof window !== 'undefined' && performance.getEntriesByType('navigation')[0]?.type !== 'reload';
  // More robust: the frame is rendered inside the @modal slot only when interception happened, so the
  // component can simply check whether it is inside a dialog context provided by the layout.
  return (
    <Dialog open onClose={() => router.back()}      // Back, not 'replace to the feed': Back must close the modal
                                                     // and land on the feed, and the feed must still be there.
      label={`Photo ${params.id}`}>
      <img src={`/photos/${params.id}/full`} alt="" />
    </Dialog>
  );
}
// Rules that make modal routing correct:
//  Â· Close with history.back() (or a `replace` to the underlying page) so the URL and history stay coherent.
//  Â· A "close" must restore focus to the trigger (4.5.7) and be reachable by Escape.
//  Â· When the route is entered by a HARD navigation, `default.tsx` in @modal renders instead, so the user
//    gets a full page (shareable, indexable) â€” this is the fallback that makes the pattern safe.
//  Â· The underlying slot must not re-fetch when the modal opens, or you get a visible refetch flash behind the dialog.
```

**The React Router / Remix equivalent** (no parallel slots; you get the same UX with a URL convention):

```tsx
// Convention: a modal is a route with a `?modal=` (or a `layout` sibling) that renders over the current view.
// React Router's nested-routes + location.state gives you interception semantics manually:
const routes = [
  { path: '/gallery', element: <GalleryLayout />, children: [
      { index: true, element: <Gallery /> },
      // The "intercepted" variant lives at the SAME path but reads the URL marker.
      { path: 'photo/:id', element: <PhotoRoute /> },
  ]},
];

function GalleryLayout() {
  const location = useLocation();
  const params = useParams();
  const isModal = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('modal') === 'photo';
  // Soft nav: /gallery?modal=photo&photo=42   -> render <PhotoRoute> as a dialog, gallery still mounted
  // Hard nav: /gallery/photo/42              -> render <PhotoRoute> as a full page
  return (
    <>
      <Outlet />
      {isModal && <Dialog onClose={() => navigate(-1)}><PhotoRoute id={params.id} /></Dialog>}
    </>
  );
}

// Progressively enhanced, no-JS form of a modal route: <Form method="get" action="/gallery?modal=photo" />
// works because GET forms navigate the same way a Link does.
```

**Why `default.tsx` (parallel-route fallback) is mandatory** â€” this is the interview-worthy detail. A parallel slot must render *something* on every navigation that does not target it. Without a `default.tsx`, a soft navigation to a route outside the slot's match throws "missing default.js / notFound". The fallback content should be a cheap placeholder (a skeleton, or nothing at all) â€” not a duplicate of the real content, because then you fetch twice.

**Trade-offs.** Parallel routes buy independent loading/error boundaries and independent refresh semantics inside one layout â€” genuinely useful for dashboards (a slow "usage" widget must not block the feed) and for authenticated chrome. They cost: the slot props are unusual and easy to get wrong, the fallback requirement is easy to miss, and debuggers show duplicated fetches when a slot renders on a navigation it should not have. Intercepting routes buy the best modal UX in web apps; they cost the most complex routing mental model in any framework, two code paths per modal (soft and hard), and the requirement that every modal be a *real route* with its own data loading â€” which is more work than a component, and exactly why it is worth it (the alternative is a modal that cannot be shared or server-rendered).

**When not to use them.** Don't intercept if the "modal" is genuinely transient (a confirm dialog, a tooltip, a zoom â€” use a component and a portal; 4.1.16). Don't use parallel slots for two things that always load together â€” use `Promise.all` in one component and one Suspense boundary; slots are for *independent* lifecycles. Don't use `@slot` for something that is not part of the same URL (it is not addressable; if it needs an address, it is a route).

**Production and operational.** Give every slot its own `loading.tsx`/`error.tsx` so one failing slot does not blank the layout. Watch for double-fetching across slots (two slots reading the same entity: use the query cache to dedupe, or fetch once in the layout and pass down). Track modal opens in analytics *with* the navigation type, because "share a photo URL, land in the modal" vs "land in the full page" is a real product decision you should be measuring. Make sure the intercepted route has a real, indexable full-page equivalent â€” otherwise you have built a URL that crawlers and link unfurlers cannot render.

**Common mistakes.** Missing `default.tsx`. Closing a modal with `router.push('/gallery')` instead of `back()` (breaks Back). A modal with no full-page fallback. Focus not restored on close. Escape not handled. Slot content duplicated across slots (double fetch). Intercepting a route that should have been a real page.

**What an interviewer probes.**
- "What problem do intercepting routes solve that a component modal cannot?"
- "Why does a parallel slot need a default file?"
- "How do you close an intercepted modal correctly with history?"
- "When would you *not* use parallel routes?"

**Mistakes:** `push` instead of `back` on close; missing slot fallbacks; using interception for simple dialogs; duplicated data fetching across slots.

