topics.referenceMarkdown:
ðŸŸ¡ Intermediate Â· **Must-know** Â· Prerequisites: 4.5.3 (segments), 4.5.6 (splitting), 4.5.10 (router choice)

**Definition:** **File-based routing** derives the route table from the directory tree â€” the filesystem *is* the router configuration. Add a file, get a route; delete a file, the route is gone; move a file, the URL changes. Nothing declares a path, a layout chain, or a param list in JS, because there is no second place where the URL can be written down and drift out of sync. The important part is that a convention is only as good as its escape hatches: **route groups**, **layout files**, **dynamic segments**, **optional catch-alls**, and a documented way to opt out (`routes.ts`).

**Why it exists â€” the problem it solves.** Central problems in a large app: (1) the URL is written in two places â€” config and code â€” so they drift; (2) adding a route touches a shared config file, so every feature PR conflicts in the same file; (3) a param rename breaks silently because no type connects the segment to the component's props; (4) nobody can tell what URLs exist without grepping the config. A convention makes the route set *enumerable* (`ls app/`), gives the compiler something to check, and lets a plugin generate types, so a bad `navigate({ to: '/invoice/$id', params: {} })` is a build error instead of a 404 in production.

**Mechanics â€” Next.js App Router (segments and groups):**

```
app/
  layout.tsx                     // root layout: <html>, <body>  â€” required
  page.tsx                       // "/"  -> re-exports the group page
  (marketing)/                   // ROUTE GROUP: parentheses = NO url segment
    layout.tsx                   //   layout for marketing only, url unchanged
    page.tsx                     //   still "/"  (the marketing homepage)
    pricing/page.tsx             //   "/pricing"
  (app)/                         // second group, same "/" â€” used to keep auth layouts apart
    layout.tsx                   //   authenticated shell
    dashboard/page.tsx           //   "/dashboard"
  invoices/
    layout.tsx                   // layout for /invoices/* â€” does NOT remount on child nav
    page.tsx                     // "/invoices"
    loading.tsx                  // Suspense fallback for this segment
    error.tsx                    // 'use client' â€” error boundary for THIS segment only
    not-found.tsx                // 404 UI for this segment
    [invoiceId]/                 // dynamic segment -> "/invoices/:invoiceId"
      page.tsx                   //   params: { invoiceId: string }  (await params in Next 15)
      @details/                  // parallel slot -> rendered into the parent layout's `details` prop
        page.tsx
    [...filters]/                // CATCH-ALL  -> "/invoices/a/b/c"   (one or more segments)
      page.tsx
    [[...filters]]/              // OPTIONAL CATCH-ALL -> also matches "/invoices"  (zero or more)
      page.tsx

// URL produced by the tree above:
//   /                  /pricing              /dashboard
//   /invoices          /invoices/:id         /invoices/a/b/c
// Rules:
//  Â· Two files may NOT resolve to the same URL without a group to disambiguate (app/page.tsx AND (marketing)/page.tsx
//    both claim "/" -> build error). Groups exist to solve exactly this.
//  Â· A layout persists across navigation to its children; only its `key`-scoped state resets. Put state in a
//    layout and it survives child navigation â€” that is a feature (a filter panel) and a bug source (a stale cart).
//  Â· `loading.tsx` / `error.tsx` / `not-found.tsx` are per SEGMENT, so the blast radius of a failure is one segment.
```

**Mechanics â€” TanStack Router (code-first but file-generated):**

```
src/routes/
  __root.tsx                      // root route: <Outlet/> + the single error boundary + providers
  index.tsx                       // "/"
  invoices/
    route.tsx                     // this file IS the route: path, loader, validateSearch, component
    index.tsx                     // "/invoices"
    $invoiceId/
      route.tsx                   // "/invoices/$invoiceId" â€” $ is the param marker
  posts/
    $postId/
      edit/
        route.tsx

// route.tsx is fully typed; the plugin generates a route tree and registers it:
```

```tsx
// src/routes/invoices/$invoiceId/route.tsx
import { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'
import { z } from 'zod'

export const Route = createFileRoute('/invoices/$invoiceId')({
  validateSearch: zodValidator(searchSchema),   // search params validated -> inferred types
  loaderDeps: ({ search }) => ({ status: search.status }),   // ONLY these deps re-run the loader
  loader: ({ params, deps, context }) =>
    context.api.getInvoice(params.invoiceId, deps.status),
  component: InvoiceRoute,
  errorComponent: RouteError,        // per-route boundary
  notFoundComponent: () => <p>No such invoice</p>,
})

function InvoiceRoute() {
  const { invoiceId } = Route.useParams()     // typed from the path â€” rename-proof
  const { status, page } = Route.useSearch()  // typed from the Zod schema
  const invoice = Route.useLoaderData()
  // navigate() is checked: `router.navigate({ to: '/invoices/$invoiceId', params: { invoiceId } })`
  // fails to COMPILE if the path or a param name changed.
}
```

```tsx
// vite.config.ts â€” the plugin is what makes the convention real (it scans, generates, and type-checks)
import { tanstackRouter } from '@tanstack/router-plugin/vite'
export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react(), tanstackRouter({ target: 'react', autoCodeSplitting: true })],
})
// `autoCodeSplitting` also splits the bundle per route file (4.5.6) for free.
```

```tsx
// The escape hatch: keep a hand-written tree and register it explicitly, for anything the convention can't express.
import { routeTree } from './routeTree.gen'      // generated
import { rootRoute } from './routes/__root'
import { adminRoute } from './routes/admin'       // hand-written, imported by the generator's config

export function registerRoutes(router: Router) {
  router.routeTree = router.routeTree.addChildren([adminRoute])   // merged into the generated tree
}
// Rule: at most ONE place may add to the route tree, and it must be documented. Two writers = two sources of truth.
```

**Trade-offs.** File-based routing buys: no config file to merge-conflict, an enumerable route set, automatic per-route code splitting, and â€” when codegen is involved â€” compile-time checking of paths, params, and search. It costs: a build step you did not write, a learning curve for the conventions (group vs folder, `[id]` vs `:id` vs `$id`), the fact that **moving a file changes a URL** (so "just reorganise" becomes a production incident without redirects), limited expressiveness for anything irregular (deeply conditional matching, versioned APIs with a custom ranking function â€” which is exactly why TanStack Router lets you drop to a code-defined route, and why Next lets you write a `route.ts` handler outside the tree). The rule of thumb I give teams: **convention for the 90%, an explicit escape hatch for the 10%, and never both for the same route.**

**Production and operational.** Route renames need permanent redirects â€” a `redirects()` map in the router config plus 301s from the edge, tested, because the URLs are in emails, bookmarks and QR codes forever. Watch the build time: codegen over a very large app is a real cost, and a slow build blocks CI. Audit the route list in CI (`routeTree` â†’ a snapshot file, diffed in review) so an accidental `mkdir` cannot silently publish an unprotected or unstyled route. And keep auth at the loader/edge layer (4.5.5), because "this route is in a `(private)` folder" is organisation, not enforcement.

**Common mistakes.** Two files resolving to the same URL without a group; assuming a group creates a path segment (it does not); putting request state in a layout and wondering why it survives navigation; using `(group)` for auth instead of a real check; renaming a folder without redirects; mixing generated and hand-written routes without a documented single writer; and relying on the folder name for security.

**What an interviewer probes.**
- "What do route groups do, and when do you need one?"
- "How do you keep a route rename from breaking production?"
- "What does codegen buy you that a JS route array cannot?"
- "Where do you draw the line between convention and configuration?"
- "How would you audit which routes exist and which are protected?"

**Mistakes:** treating the convention as enforcement; no redirect discipline on moves; two writers for the route tree; ignoring that optional catch-all can swallow its siblings' URLs.

