## 4.6.11.5 Google Docs-style collaborative editor

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know** (asked as "how would you build X" and as the classic "this is a multi-month project" honesty test)

**Scope honesty first.** State it in your first 30 seconds: *this is a multi-month project; in an interview I will scope it to the hard parts and the trade-offs.* Then design within that scope. Interviewers reward this framing more than a confident fake design.

**Requirements (scoped).** Multi-user text editing with live presence, conflict-free convergence, undo/redo, autosave and version history, comments. **Out of scope for the interview answer:** tables, images, equations, drawing, offline-first sync, printing, plugin API, and mobile editing.

**The core problem: convergence.** Two families of algorithms:

| | OT (Operational Transform) | CRDT |
|---|---|---|
| Idea | Transform each operation against concurrent operations so all replicas converge | Represent data as a conflict-free structure; merge is commutative/idempotent, so order does not matter |
| Central piece | A **transformation function** per operation pair, e.g. `t_i(insert(5, 'A')) â†’ insert(5 + |L|)` | A sequence CRDT (RGA) with unique IDs per character |
| Server | **Central authoritative server** that orders operations and broadcasts transformed ops | Any peer; ops can arrive in any order |
| Rich text | Hard (formatting ops interact with ranges; needs per-attribute transforms) | Easier conceptually (per-attribute LWW with proper merges) |
| Cursor/presence | Position transforms are fiddly (needs offset â†’ position mapping) | Native: positions are CRDT positions |
| Offline | Requires server authority and a reconnect protocol | Natural fit (merge on reconnect) |
| Cost to build | Weeks per operation type, and every new op type needs a transform | Months to get a production-quality sequence CRDT |
| Used by | Google Docs (operational transform family) | Figma, and most modern collaborative editors |

**Interview answer structure:** "I would use a CRDT (RGA-style sequence CRDT with unique character IDs and per-attribute last-writer-wins registers), because presence/cursors come for free and offline merging is tractable, accepting a higher memory cost per document (tombstones) and a larger initial build. I would keep a central server for auth, document membership, persistence, and snapshotting, but it would not need to order operations. If we only ever need online collaboration with a single central authority, OT is cheaper to ship."

**Presence and cursors.** Separate channel from document content (never persist it, never version it, never CRDT it). Send throttled (~30â€“60 Hz, and only on change) relative positions (e.g. `{ leftAnchor: 'char-id-8a3f', offset: 3 }`) rather than absolute offsets â€” a relative position survives concurrent edits; an absolute offset does not. Decay presence (a cursor with no update for 10 s disappears), and send an explicit `leave` on unload so cursors do not litter the document.

**Undo/redo semantics â€” the subtle part.** Naive per-user undo stacks break: undoing your own insert after someone else inserted inside it must not delete their text. Correct approach: record undo entries with **intent ranges** ("the run of text I typed") and undo by deleting the surviving characters within that range, not by replaying inverse operations against current state. With a CRDT this is a range deletion over currently-surviving IDs; with OT it is a transformed inverse. Only undo *your own* actions (collaborative editors do not offer "undo what she did"). Keep the stack bounded (50â€“100 entries) and clear redo on a new local action after an undo.

**Autosave and versioning.** Snapshots, not a save button: server persists updates to an append-only log with a periodic compacted snapshot (e.g. every 100 updates or 10 s of inactivity); version history is created on explicit milestones and on a schedule (keep last N versions plus named versions); every snapshot is independently loadable so recovery does not require replaying the whole log. Compaction requires tombstone GC â€” which is safe only once all clients have acknowledged past that version (track client clocks and only GC beyond the minimum acknowledged cursor). Include document-level metadata (owner, ACL, created) in the CRDT root so membership changes merge consistently.

**Comments and suggestions.** Model them as **anchored ranges referencing stable CRDT IDs** with a separate comment thread entity; a comment survives concurrent edits because its anchor is relative. Suggestion mode = a proposed range with the replacement text and an author; acceptance applies a real document edit (so it goes through the CRDT and converges) rather than mutating the document behind the CRDT's back. This is a genuinely good design point to say out loud: *suggestions are data; acceptance is an operation.*

**Large-document performance.** Virtualise rendering (only the viewport's text nodes; measure with `IntersectionObserver` on block elements); keep the CRDT document out of the DOM (never render a whole document as DOM nodes); avoid re-creating the editor model on every keystroke; batch remote operations (a microtask or rAF drain, and coalesce remote cursor updates); do not re-parse HTML for the whole document on any local change; consider block-level granularity in the model so a paragraph edit does not dirty the whole document. Budget: typing latency â‰¤ 50 ms for a 20k-word document on a mid-tier laptop; remote ops applied within 200 ms of arrival.

**Offline.** With a CRDT you can hold updates in IndexedDB and flush on reconnect â€” but you must handle the hard cases: editing the same region while offline produces a merge that is technically correct and *semantically* ugly (that is the honest limitation), and access revocation while offline (deny on reconnect and roll back). Say both out loud.

**Accessibility (underrated, and a differentiator).** Collaborative editors are hard for screen-reader users because of constant cursor movement. Provide: a keyboard-accessible command palette for formatting (never mouse-only toolbars), an "accessible view" mode that exposes a linearised document, proper roles for the text body (`role="textbox"` with `aria-multiline` on a contenteditable wrapper, or a textarea-backed custom editor for true a11y), announced collaborator events (`aria-live="polite"`: "Alex joined"), and never let remote cursor movements steal focus or scroll the viewport (a classic bug). Contrast remote cursors on selection and don't rely on colour alone (label each collaborator with a name).

**Multiplayer testing.** Deterministic simulation: seed a document, generate N random concurrent operations, apply them in many different orders, and assert convergence (all replicas identical) â€” this is the test that matters and it is cheap to write with a property-based approach. Then test intent-undo semantics, and test the resync path with an injected packet loss.

**Follow-ups to expect.** CRDT vs OT (above). How do you handle a very large document (10 MB)? (Block granularity, snapshotting, lazy loading of unseen blocks.) How do you implement undo (above). How do you handle a user editing while offline (above). How do you scale the fan-out (server holds the doc state, broadcasts deltas; partition by document on a broker; a snapshotter per doc; Phase 12). How would you scope this for a 3-person team in a quarter (shared text only, no rich formatting, presence as coloured text, no comments; ship single-user editing with CRDT data structures from day one so you do not have to migrate).

**What breaks at scale.** Per-document memory server-side (mitigate: snapshot + delta, GC tombstones beyond the acknowledged cursor); CRDT growth from tombstones and metadata (mitigate: compaction with GC, per-user undo depth limits); a hot document with hundreds of concurrent editors (mitigate: partition by document, per-doc lock-free ordering not required by CRDT but snapshot rate must be tuned, and awareness fan-out is a per-doc pub/sub); the awareness channel at 60 Hz Ã— 500 users = 30k messages/second per document (mitigate: throttle per user, send cursors only for users in the viewport â€” "cursor interest management", which is what real editors do).

---

## 4.6.11.6 Analytics dashboard

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know**

**Requirements.** Show KPIs and trends over selectable date ranges, filterable by dimension, with drill-down, table + chart visualisation of large datasets, export, optional real-time updates, role-based visibility, and fast enough to be usable at 100k rows.

**Layout and information hierarchy.**
```
â”Œ Filter bar: [date range] [compare to previous] [segment â–¾] [saved views] [export] [live âŸ³]
â”œ KPI row:    Revenue   Orders   AOV   Conv rate        (value + delta vs comparison + sparkline)
â”œ Charts row: Time series (primary) | Breakdown by dimension (top 8 + "other")
â”œ Table:      Virtualised, sortable, column-pinned, per-column formatting
â”” Detail:     Click a row â†’ drill-down panel (or route) â†’ entity page
```
Hierarchy rules to state: one primary question per view, the KPI row answers "is it normal?", the time series answers "when did it change?", the breakdown answers "which segment?", and the table answers "which rows?". Put the date range and comparison selector in a single consistent place, and make the comparison period explicit (`vs previous 28 days`) â€” an unlabelled delta is a misreading generator.

**Data-fetching strategy.** Three distinct data shapes, three strategies:
- **KPIs and time series:** one aggregate endpoint with `from`, `to`, `granularity`, `filters[]`, `compare=true`. Aggregates server-side, always. Never ship raw events to the browser to aggregate 100k rows.
- **Tables:** server-side pagination *and* sorting (`sort=revenue.desc`, cursor) â€” the browser must never hold 100k rows to sort them. Virtualise the 50 rows it does hold.
- **Entity detail:** route-level fetch on drill-down.
Use a query cache keyed by the full filter set, with `staleTime` per surface (KPIs 30 s, tables 60 s, exports server-side). Prefetch the default view on route mount. Parallelise independent KPI requests but cap concurrency; show skeletons per card (so the page is useful before all data arrives) rather than one full-page spinner. Cancel superseded requests when filters change (the stale-response race again) and ignore out-of-order results.

**Caching and consistency.** Warehouse queries are slow (seconds), so: materialised aggregates for common ranges, a precomputed cube/aggregate table per (tenant, day, dimensions), and cache aggressively with a `dataVersion` per tenant-day so all users of a tenant see consistent numbers for a given period (this matters â€” mismatched numbers between two views destroy trust). Push invalidation when late-arriving data changes a past day, and show "data as of HH:MM" for freshness. Never cache per-user filtered queries across users.

**Timezones and date ranges.** Decisive interview point: the dashboard's timezone is a *product* decision. Store timestamps in UTC; support (a) a fixed reporting timezone (e.g. the tenant's billing timezone, which makes daily totals stable), and (b) a user/browser-local view with an explicit label. Aggregate in the **reporting timezone** server-side, because "yesterday's revenue" in the reporting zone must not shift when a user in another zone looks. Support comparison periods of equal length, "last 28 days", month-to-date, quarter-to-date, and custom ranges with validation (from â‰¤ to, max span, no future dates). Use the platform `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for labels and an ISO week policy you state explicitly.

**Real-time updates.** Polling vs WebSocket, and the trade-offs:
- **Polling** (e.g. every 30 s on the live tiles only): simple, stateless, works everywhere, slightly stale, costs request volume per viewer.
- **WebSocket/SSE** push: lower latency and cost per viewer at scale, but you now have connection management, ordering, and reconnect (4.6.11.4).
Say the threshold: below ~50 concurrent viewers per tenant, polling is the right call; above that, push. Never push the entire dashboard â€” push *delta* updates to a few tiles and revalidate the rest. Also: pause updates when the tab is hidden and show "paused" rather than pretending to be live. Aggregate server-side with a small time window (5â€“10 s buckets) so the update rate is bounded regardless of user count.

**Performance budget for 100k rows.**
- Never transfer 100k rows. Server-side pagination/sorting + aggregation. If a full export is needed, do it server-side as a job (generate a CSV asynchronously, email a signed link) â€” a 100k-row CSV is not a browser download.
- Virtualise the visible ~40 rows; fixed row height (or measured with `ResizeObserver`) so scrolling is O(visible).
- Keep the chart bounded: aggregate to â‰¤ 500 points per series (bucket by hour/day as needed) and draw on canvas (or an SVG with a small point count) â€” a 100k-point SVG path will jank.
- Formatting is a real cost: `Intl.NumberFormat` instances are expensive, so create them once per locale/currency and memoise the formatted values per cell. Precompute formatting in a worker if you must.
- Budget: KPI row visible in < 1 s (cached), table interactive in < 2 s, scroll at 60 fps, filter change reflected in < 1.5 s.
- Isolation: a slow aggregate must not block the filter bar â€” fetch per card with independent skeletons.

**Loading / empty / error states â€” this is graded heavily.**
- **Loading:** per-card skeletons with the final layout dimensions (zero CLS); a subtle top progress line for subsequent refetches so the user can keep working with stale-but-labelled data ("updated 12 s ago").
- **Empty:** distinguish *no data yet* (onboarding: "no events in this period", plus "view last 30 days"), *filtered to nothing* (name the filter and offer "clear filters"), and *delayed data* (ETL lag message).
- **Error:** per-card error with Retry and a correlation id; do not blank the whole page because one chart failed. Distinguish permission errors ("ask an admin for access") from query errors (Retry) from timeouts ("this range is too large, narrow it") â€” and **degrade gracefully**: on a timeout for a heavy range, return a coarser granularity and say so rather than showing nothing.
- **Stale data:** label it. A dashboard that shows old numbers without saying so is worse than one that errors.

**Accessibility.** Charts must be understandable without colour: also encode series by line style/marker/label, provide a data table alternative for the primary chart, and use an accessible name plus a summary ("Revenue rose 12% week over week, driven by the US segment"). Tables: real `<table>` semantics with `<th scope>`, caption, sortable columns exposing `aria-sort`, and focusable interactive cells with names ("Revenue for ACME: $12,340, row 3 of 40"). The live region announces refreshes politely ("Data updated"), never on every cell. Ensure keyboard access to filters, date picker (4.6.2), and drill-down. Respect reduced motion for chart transitions. Contrast: dashboard colour palettes must pass AA on both themes (4.6.1).

**API design.**
```
GET /v1/analytics/kpis?from=&to=&tz=&compare=prev&filters[segment]=us&granularity=day
200 { "dataVersion":"2026-02-01.3", "asOf":"â€¦", "kpis":[{key,value,unit,delta,series:[â€¦]}],
      "cache":{"maxAge":30} }
GET /v1/analytics/rows?from=&to=&sort=revenue.desc&cursor=&limit=50
200 { "rows":[â€¦], "pageInfo":{endCursor,hasNextPage}, "totalCount": 128341 }
POST /v1/analytics/exports { from, to, filters, format } â†’ 202 { jobId }
GET /v1/analytics/exports/{jobId} â†’ { status, url?, expiresAt }
```
Keep the filter grammar consistent (`filters[k]=v` repeated) and validate it server-side; a dashboard API that accepts arbitrary expressions is an injection and a performance hazard.

**Edge cases.** A date range with no traffic; a filter that excludes the user's own row (permissions); a 10Ã— outlier making the y-axis useless (log scale option, or clamp with a note); a metric that changed definition mid-period (annotate the chart at the change date â€” a very strong answer); a very long tenant name breaking the header; a user with access to no segments (show the "request access" state); timezone of the reporting zone differing from the user's; comparison period containing incomplete data (grey it out); an export of 5M rows (job, not request); a dashboard shared as a URL with filters â€” encode the filter set in the URL so links are shareable and reproducible (also makes bug reports actionable).

**Follow-ups to expect.** How would you make this real-time? (Push deltas to tiles; polling under a viewer threshold.) How do you handle a metric definition change? (Version the metric, annotate, keep both, backfill.) How do you test this? (Component tests for the empty/error/permission states â€” most of the value; E2E for the drill-down; a11y checks on the table; visual regression for the KPI row.) How would you optimise for a tenant with 50k users watching one dashboard? (Materialised aggregate + push; a shared per-tenant cached response with a short TTL; compute the aggregation once per 10 s window, not once per viewer.)

**What breaks at scale.** Warehouse fan-out per viewer (mitigate: aggregate tables, a caching layer keyed by (tenant, range, filters), and per-window computation); long ranges over huge fact tables (mitigate: precomputed daily rollups + a max-range policy with coarse bucketing); 100k-row exports (mitigate: async jobs + signed URLs + retention); many concurrent live viewers (mitigate: push fan-out per document, not per viewer); and permissions â€” per-row filtering in the warehouse (mitigate: mandatory predicate injection server-side, never client-side filtering of a full result set).

