## 4.6.11.1 Typeahead / autocomplete

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know**

**Requirements.** Given a prefix, return ranked suggestions in under ~100 ms perceived; keyboard and screen-reader operable; supports prefix search over a catalogue plus "recent searches" and "popular searches"; optional typeahead segmentation; min query length; rate-limit and error resilience; optional personalisation and cross-device history.

**Scale numbers to say out loud.** 2M daily users, ~20% use search per session, ~8 keystrokes per query â†’ ~1.3M queries/minute peak-ish; catalogue 20M entities. Per keystroke we can afford one request; we cannot afford a request per keystroke *per suggestion source*.

**Client state**
```ts
type SearchState =
  | { kind: 'idle' }
  | { kind: 'typing'; query: string }
  | { kind: 'too-short'; minLength: number }
  | { kind: 'loading'; query: string; requestId: number; stale: Suggestion[] }
  | { kind: 'ready'; query: string; requestId: number; groups: SuggestionGroup[] }
  | { kind: 'error'; query: string; requestId: number; retryable: boolean; stale: Suggestion[] };
```
- One request at a time, identified by an incrementing `requestId`; **responses whose `requestId` is not the latest are discarded** (the canonical stale-response race). Cancel the previous request too â€” `AbortController` â€” because cancelling frees the connection and stops wasted server work.
- `query` (raw), `debouncedQuery` (after debounce), and `results` are separate fields. Conflating them is the bug behind "the list flickers while typing".
- Cached responses keyed by normalised query (an LRU of 50) make backspace-free navigation instant and turn a repeat query into no network call.
- Recent searches in a small store (persisted), popular searches from the server, history cross-device if the user is signed in.

**Key flows**
```ts
async function onChange(raw: string) {
  setState({ kind: 'typing', query: raw });
  const q = raw.trim();
  if (q.length < MIN_LENGTH) { abortInFlight(); return setState({ kind: 'too-short', minLength: MIN_LENGTH }); }
  const requestId = ++seq.current;
  debouncedAbort?.abort();
  debouncedAbort = new AbortController();
  await sleep(DEBOUNCE_MS, debouncedAbort.signal);        // 120â€“180 ms
  const cacheKey = normalise(q);
  const hit = cache.get(cacheKey);
  if (hit) return setState({ kind: 'ready', query: raw, requestId, groups: hit });

  inFlight?.abort();                                        // supersede the previous one
  inFlight = new AbortController();
  try {
    const res = await api.suggest(q, { signal: inFlight.signal });
    if (requestId !== seq.current) return;                 // stale: drop silently
    cache.set(cacheKey, res.groups);
    setState({ kind: 'ready', query: raw, requestId, groups: res.groups });
  } catch (err) {
    if (isAbort(err)) return;
    setState({ kind: 'error', query: raw, requestId, retryable: true, stale: previousGroups });
  }
}
```
**Debounce vs throttle.** Debounce (wait for quiet) is right for "user is still typing". Throttle (max rate) is right for guaranteed freshness, e.g. a live-updating result count. Practical combo: debounce ~150 ms, plus a hard cap so a fast typist cannot queue requests (leading-edge execution for the first keystroke, then debounced). Also: cancel rather than merely ignore, and ignore out-of-order responses â€” you need **both**.

**Sources and ranking (client's part).** The client composes three sources in one response-shape contract: `prefix` (server, ranked), `recent` (local store, filtered by prefix, capped at 5), `popular` (server, only when the query is empty or the prefix search returns nothing). Ranking is a server concern (Phase 12 â†’ search autocomplete); the client's job is to render the grouping and keep the section order stable so keyboard position does not jump. If you rank client-side, you need the fields the rank depends on (`popularity`, `recency`, `matchQuality`) in the payload â€” say that explicitly.

**Performance budget.** Debounce 150 ms + request p50 40 ms / p95 120 ms + render â‰¤ 16 ms â†’ perceived latency under ~300 ms, with the previous result visible while loading (never a blank box). Keep the DOM tiny (cap at 10 suggestions, virtualise only if you show more), debounce *prefetch on hover/arrow* if you want perceived instant selection, and never block input on the request.

**Loading / empty / error.** Loading: keep the last results visible with a subtle progress indicator (no spinner replacing content â€” it destroys the user's context and causes layout shift). Empty: two different empties â€” "no matches for *x*" with alternatives (did you mean, browse categories) and "we found nothing recent", which is an invitation to search. Error: if you have stale results, keep them and show a non-blocking inline notice with Retry; if you have nothing, show a Retry affordance; on 429, respect `Retry-After`, stop the auto-retry, and tell the user to slow down rather than spinning. Rate-limit handling is the most-missed flow in interviews.

**Accessibility.** This is the canonical combobox pattern and it is graded strictly:
```jsx
<label id="q-label" htmlFor="q">Search</label>
<input
  id="q" role="combobox" aria-expanded={open} aria-controls="q-listbox"
  aria-autocomplete="list" aria-activedescendant={activeId}
  aria-describedby="q-hint"
  autoComplete="off" spellCheck={false}
/>
<ul id="q-listbox" role="listbox" aria-label="Suggestions">
  {groups.map(g => (
    <li key={g.name} role="presentation">
      <div id={`g-${g.name}`} role="presentation">{g.name}</div>
      <ul role="group" aria-labelledby={`g-${g.name}`}>
        {g.items.map(it => (
          <li key={it.id} id={`opt-${it.id}`} role="option"
              aria-selected={it.id === activeId}>{it.label}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
```
- **DOM focus stays in the input**; the visual active option is referenced by `aria-activedescendant` (this is the whole point of the pattern, and it is what most candidates get wrong).
- Keyboard: `â†“`/`â†‘` move the active option (with wrap), `Enter` selects, `Esc` closes (and clears the active option), `Tab` accepts-and-moves-on per spec nuance, `Home`/`End` jump, `PgUp`/`PgDn` by 5â€“10.
- Announce result count with a polite live region (`aria-live="polite"`), and do **not** announce on every keystroke â€” debounce the announcement or it becomes unusable.
- Results must not be focusable (no `tabindex` on options); `role="option"` inside `role="listbox"`; selected state via `aria-selected`.
- Do not use `aria-selected` for "highlighted" *and* "selected" simultaneously; the active-descendant is the highlight.
- Form submit: `Enter` with no active option submits the raw query (a common, expected behaviour).
- Labels: the input needs a real `<label>`; error and hint text via `aria-describedby`.

**Caching.** Client LRU by normalised query (invalidate on data-version change); server/CDN cache popular-prefix responses briefly (tens of seconds) with stale-while-revalidate; long-lived cache only for "popular searches" (minutesâ€“hours). Recent searches are local, capped, and cleared on consent withdrawal or logout.

**API design.**
```
GET /v1/suggest?q=pho&limit=8&locale=en-GB&session=<opaque>
200 { "version": "...", "groups": [
        { "name": "Products", "items": [{ "id","label","subtitle","score","matchRanges" }] },
        { "name": "Recent", "items": [...] } ] }
429 { "code":"rate_limited", "retryAfterMs": 1500 }
```
- Server must be **authoritative for ranking** and return `matchRanges` so the client can highlight without re-implementing matching.
- Include a data `version`/etag so the client can drop stale caches after a catalogue change.
- Prefix requests should be idempotent, cacheable, and cheap server-side (trie/prefix lookup with a bounded fan-out and a popularity prior â€” Phase 12).

**Edge cases.** Empty query (show popular + recent); one character (min length 2 unless the catalogue demands 1); whitespace-only; unicode/emoji/normalisation (NFC) and diacritic-insensitive matching; very long paste (cap at 200 chars, debounce once); rapid delete-then-retype; tab-away then back (stale UI); offline (cache last results or a message â€” never a spinner forever); `Accept-Language`/locale changes; duplicate labels with different ids (key by id, never label); a result that disappeared server-side between render and select (handle 404 on select gracefully); the same query from two tabs; rate limits keyed by IP vs user (design for both).

**Follow-up questions to expect.** How would you rank? (popularity prior + match quality + personalisation + freshness + business boosts.) How do you handle misspellings? (edit distance / phonetic over a prefix trie; or a client-side "did you mean" from a separate service.) How do you test this? (unit tests for the stale-response logic with fake timers; integration test asserting `aria-activedescendant` moves; e2e with a mocked API; load test the ranking service.) How do you measure success? (search-to-click rate, zero-results rate, time-to-first-click, conversion.)

**What breaks at scale.** Ranking service latency dominates p95 (mitigate: per-key request coalescing, a bloom/trie in front of the DB, a cached popularity table); the client's LRU grows unbounded (cap it); the suggestion payload grows (cap `limit`, trim fields); every keystroke from every user multiplies backend QPS (mitigate: debounce + cache + the CDN); and personalisation state grows per user (mitigate: compute a small per-user "affinity vector" offline and merge at query time, never a synchronous join).

---

## 4.6.11.2 Infinite feed

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know**

**Requirements.** Endless scroll of a ranked feed with stable ordering, no duplicates, scroll restoration across navigation, back/forward correctness, prefetch near the sentinel, real empty/end state, analytics that survive batching, bounded memory.

**State model.** The important insight: **items, cursors, and scroll position are three separate concerns and the bugs come from conflating them.**
```ts
type Feed<T> = {
  items: T[];                 // ordered, de-duplicated by id
  pageInfo: { hasNextPage: boolean; endCursor: string | null };
  status: 'initial' | 'loadingNext' | 'loadingPrev' | 'refreshing' | 'done' | 'error';
  error: { code: string; retryable: boolean } | null;
  seen: Set<string>;          // id dedupe; or rely on indexOf for small lists
  scrollAnchors: Map<string, number>;  // id -> offset, for restoration
};
```
- **Cursor pagination, not offset.** `offset` pagination duplicates and skips items when the feed mutates (a post deleted or a new post inserted above you) â€” the single most important sentence in this design. Cursors are stable but "load older posts" is *prepending*, which needs a *backward* cursor.
- **Dedupe by id on insert**, always. Under realtime insertion, the server may return an item you already have; `mergeById(newItems, existing)` keeps order and uniqueness.
- **Dedupe on re-render** is about keys: use stable `key={item.id}`, never `key={index}` â€” with prepend-heavy feeds, index keys produce the classic "input resets / items appear to shuffle" bug.

**Key flows.**
- Load next: `IntersectionObserver` on a sentinel with `rootMargin: '800px'` (prefetch before visible), `disabled` while a page is in flight, and a guard that `hasNextPage` is false (otherwise the sentinel re-triggers forever).
- Load previous: a *second* observer near the top with `rootMargin: '-200px'` or an explicit "New posts" button (better a11y, and it avoids surprise jumps).
- **Scroll restoration** is the hard part. Two-part approach: (1) store the id of the top visible item plus its pixel offset when leaving the route; (2) on return, render until that id exists, then `scrollTo(id.offset - measuredOffset)`. Restoring by raw `scrollY` breaks because the DOM height differs while items are still loading. Combine with `history.scrollRestoration = 'manual'` so the browser does not fight you.
```ts
// Restoration: measure after layout, not on mount
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const anchor = savedAnchor.current; if (!anchor) return;
  const el = listRef.current?.querySelector(`[data-id="${anchor.id}"]`);
  if (!el) return;                                     // item not loaded yet: keep top-loading
  const delta = el.getBoundingClientRect().top - anchor.top;
  if (Math.abs(delta) > 1) listRef.current?.scrollBy(0, delta);
}, [items.length]);
```
- **Prefetch** on `pointerenter` of a visible card (or on `IntersectionObserver` at 50% visibility) to warm the detail API â€” cheap and invisible.
- **Refresh**: re-fetch page one, merge by id, and diff to decide whether to keep the user's scroll position (if the top items are unchanged, do not move; if they changed, show a "N new posts" pill).

**Performance budget.** Budget for the DOM, not the network: virtualise after ~100â€“200 rows (windowing with fixed/estimated row heights, or `content-visibility: auto` with `contain-intrinsic-size` as a cheap 80% solution). Target: 60 fps scroll with a long task budget of <50 ms per frame; images lazy below the fold with `width`/`height` set to avoid CLS; the initial page â‰¤ 20 items so first contentful paint is fast. Keep memory flat â€” a user scrolling 5,000 items should not retain 5,000 full objects.

**Loading / empty / error.** Skeletons only for the first page (a skeleton per row, matching layout, to keep CLS at 0); for subsequent pages, no spinner â€” keep the content and load silently. Empty (no items at all): onboarding with a reason to create the first item. End ("you're all caught up"): a visible footer marker so users learn the boundary exists, with a link to something to do. Error mid-scroll: an inline retry bar pinned to the failed position, never a full-screen error that loses the user's place. Refresh error: keep old content and toast.

**Accessibility.** The pattern must announce additions: a polite live region updated with "12 more posts loaded" (throttled â€” announcing every append is unusable). Provide a non-scroll alternative: a "Load more" button (visually hidden is not enough if it is unreachable â€” keep it in the DOM, focusable, and honest) so keyboard and switch users are not forced to scroll. Manage focus: when you auto-append, do **not** move focus; when the user explicitly loads more and focus was on the "Load more" button, move focus to the first new item so they continue. Cards must be links with descriptive names, and the feed container gets `role="feed"`/`aria-busy` semantics only if you implement the full pattern, otherwise use a plain list. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for scroll animations. Test that a keyboard user can reach and trigger loading without a mouse.

**Caching.** Cache page N's payload (TanStack Query `useInfiniteQuery` or an equivalent keyed by filter set) so navigating back is instant. Invalidate on filter change. The anchor map is ephemeral state kept in a module-level or session store so it survives remounts. Server/CDN: cache the first page for a few seconds (feed freshness is per-user anyway), never cache authenticated personalised pages at the CDN without varying on user.

**API design.**
```
POST /v1/feed/pages        # POST because the sort cursor is long + filters in the body
{ "filter": {â€¦}, "after": "<opaque>", "before": "<opaque>", "limit": 20 }
200 { "items": [...], "pageInfo": { "hasNextPage": true, "endCursor": "â€¦" },
      "hasPreviousPage": true, "startCursor": "â€¦" }
```
Opaque cursors (base64 of a sort key + tiebreaker) hide the schema and let you change ranking without breaking clients. Note the idempotency of paging: `after` is stable, so a retried page returns the same items.

**Edge cases.** A user scrolling while a page loads and then reversing direction (cancel the request); items deleted mid-scroll (filter ids you no longer get from the detail endpoint, or tombstone them); an item edited to change its height (anchor by id, re-measure); a session that expires mid-feed (401 â†’ refresh once, then a full-page re-auth); offline (show cached pages and an offline banner; queue nothing that mutates); rapid filter change (abort in-flight, reset `pageInfo`); the sentinel firing repeatedly because `hasNextPage` was not yet updated; images loading after scroll (reserve aspect ratio).

**Follow-ups to expect.** How do you make it work without JavaScript / with SEO? (Server-rendered paginated list with `?cursor=` in the URL, plus `IntersectionObserver` enhancement â€” a hybrid.) How do you test scroll restoration? (E2E: scroll, navigate away, navigate back, assert the same top item and offset; deterministic via fixed viewport and `?e2e=1` to disable virtualisation.) How do you handle the ordering with a realtime channel? (Insert with an anchor rule: if a new item's sort key is above the current top, show a "N new" pill; otherwise insert in place, keyed by id.)

**What breaks at scale.** Ranking joins dominate p95 (Phase 12 â†’ news feed); unbounded growth kills scroll performance and memory (virtualise + a cap with an explicit "load older from archive" escape); real-time insertion into a cursor-paginated feed is genuinely hard (the anchor model above); CDN caching is impossible for personalised feeds (accept it, cache the ranking service instead); and analytics batching on every appended page creates a burst pattern that your collector must absorb (batch with `visibilitychange`, cap queue size).

