## 4.6.11.4 Chat UI

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know**

**Requirements.** Real-time bidirectional messaging: send/receive, ordering, presence, typing, read receipts, history pagination upward, resend on failure, offline queue, optimistic UI, media upload, accessibility for live messages, bounded memory, reconnect lifecycle.

**State model â€” the important part: separate the transport from the conversation.**
```ts
type ChatState = {
  messages: Message[];        // ordered, deduped, with local + confirmed status
  outbox: OutboxItem[];       // pending/failed sends, with client-generated ids
  connection: 'idle'|'connecting'|'online'|'reconnecting'|'offline';
  presence: Record<UserId, { online: boolean; lastSeen: number }>;
  typing: Record<UserId, number>;   // value = expiry ts (auto-expiring!)
  hasMore: boolean;
};
```
- **Optimistic messages** get a `clientId` generated locally and a `status: 'sending' | 'sent' | 'failed'`. On server ack, replace by `clientId` (this is the dedupe rule). Reconnect resends unacked items with the *same* `clientId` so the server can dedupe (idempotency key) â€” this is the difference between "mostly works" and "works".
- **Ordering** is by server sequence (`seq`, monotonically increasing per conversation), not by client timestamp. Client timestamps are for display only, because clock skew between devices is a guaranteed source of misordering. Sort by `(seq ?? localCreatedAt)`.
- **Typing indicators must expire** (2â€“4 s TTL, cleared on a timer or on message arrival) or they lie forever.

**Key flows.**
- **WebSocket lifecycle:** `connecting â†’ open â†’ (heartbeat every 25â€“30 s) â†’ closing â†’ closed â†’ reconnect with exponential backoff + jitter (1 s, 2 s, 4 s â€¦ cap 30 s)`. On each successful (re)connect: **resubscribe, then resync** â€” issue a `GET /messages?since=<lastSeq>` to fetch anything missed while offline, then reconcile the outbox. Resuming a subscription without a resync is the classic "messages from the gap are gone forever" bug. Handle `visibilitychange` (close when hidden on mobile to save battery, reopen on visible), `online`/`offline` events, and page unload (send a presence-leaving beacon).
- **Optimistic send:** append immediately, mark `sending`, POST; on success mark `sent` and stamp `seq`; on failure mark `failed` with a Retry affordance, and keep the text in the input? No â€” keep it in the message (with retry), which is what real apps do.
- **Offline queue:** persist the outbox to IndexedDB so a refresh does not lose an unsent message; flush on reconnect; show a clear "1 message not sent" affordance rather than silently retrying forever.
- **Read receipts:** server-authoritative (the server records `lastReadSeq` per user per conversation), so it works across devices. Show "Seen" for the other user's last message only when the conversation is open and the document is visible.
- **Scroll behaviour:** stick to the bottom only when the user is already near the bottom; if they scrolled up, show a "jump to latest" pill with an unread count. Never yank the viewport when a message arrives while the user is reading history.
```ts
function shouldStickToBottom(el: HTMLElement) {
  return el.scrollHeight - el.scrollTop - el.clientHeight < 80;   // 80px threshold
}
```
- **Virtualisation is mandatory** for long histories: window the message list, keep the scroll height stable while older messages load above (measure and adjust `scrollTop` by the delta), and preserve focus across prepends.

**Performance budget.** New message render â‰¤ 8 ms; scroll at 60 fps with 500 virtualised rows; appending 1 message must not re-render the whole list (memoised rows keyed by id, or a virtualiser); WebSocket frame handling on the main thread must be batched (drain the message queue in one `requestAnimationFrame`, not per frame). Incoming bursts (a busy channel) need a batch-apply policy: render the newest 50 immediately, older ones on scroll-back.

**Loading / empty / error states.** Initial history: skeleton rows with realistic heights, then content. Empty conversation: a real empty state ("Say hi to X") â€” an empty grey box is a bug. Connection lost: a persistent, non-blocking banner ("Reconnectingâ€¦") that does not block reading; **never** clear the transcript while reconnecting. Send failed: inline per-message retry. Load-more failure: a retry row pinned in place.

**Accessibility.** Live messages must be announced but not interrupt: an `aria-live="polite"` region containing only the newest message text (append a node, do not re-render a region's whole subtree â€” that re-announces everything), and use `role="log"`/`role="status"` with `aria-relevant="additions"`. Typing indicators: `aria-live="polite"` with a short message, or better, a `aria-label` on the composer ("X is typing") so it is discoverable without chatter. Every message needs a logical structure: avatar with an accessible name (or `alt=""` when the sender name is adjacent text), a timestamp with `<time dateTime>`, and the sender name as text so it is not colour-only. Keyboard: the log should be focusable (`tabIndex={0}` with `role="log"`) so keyboard users can scroll it; arrow-key navigation between messages is a nice-to-have. Reconnection banners must be `role="status"`. Do not put focus in the transcript on new messages.

**Caching.** Cache recent pages per conversation in memory (TanStack Query with `initialPageParam`), persist the last N messages to IndexedDB for offline read (encrypt if it contains sensitive content â€” cross-ref 4.6.9), never cache the WebSocket transport. Server history is authoritative; the client cache is a read-through layer.

**API design.**
```
GET  /v1/conversations/{id}/messages?before=<seq>&limit=50
POST /v1/conversations/{id}/messages          # Idempotency-Key: <clientId>
     { "clientId", "text", "attachments": [{ "id", "w","h","mime" }] }
PATCH /v1/conversations/{id}/read             { "seq" }
POST /v1/uploads (presigned, multipart, resumable)
WS   /v1/realtime   â†’ frames:
     { "t":"msg",  "seq", "conv", "clientId", "senderId", "text", "ts" }
     { "t":"ack",  "clientId", "seq" }
     { "t":"presence","userId","state","ts" }
     { "t":"typing","userId","ts" }
     { "t":"receipt","conv","userId","lastReadSeq" }
```
Every frame is `{t, ...}` with a discriminated union â€” the client switches on `t` exhaustively, so a new frame type is a compile error rather than an ignored message.

**Edge cases.** Two tabs open (both receive the message; dedupe by id so it appears once per tab; presence must count *sessions*, not users, or someone with two tabs looks "online twice"); send while typing indicator is on (clear your own indicator); resending after a long disconnect (server idempotency plus an "edited/failed" state); a 413 for a long message (client-side limit with a counter, server authoritative); attachments uploaded but the message send fails (orphan cleanup job server-side, and an explicit "retry" that reuses the uploaded id â€” cross-ref Phase 3.9 chunked upload); timezone differences in timestamps (store UTC, render local, label with the full date on day boundaries); message edit/delete propagation (server broadcasts an update; the client patches by id); translation or profanity filtering mutating your own echo (mark the client's copy as `filtered` when the server rewrote it, otherwise the user sees a different message than they sent).

**Follow-ups to expect.** Why WebSockets vs SSE vs long-polling? (Duplex + low overhead; SSE is simpler and auto-reconnects but serverâ†’client only; long-polling is universal and works through restrictive proxies.) How do you handle >1 device per user? (Session-scoped presence; per-device read state merged to max.) How do you test realtime? (A local WS server with a scripted timeline; a "chaos" test that kills the socket at each step of a message send; a resync test asserting no gaps after a forced reconnect.) How do you reduce bandwidth? (Binary/protobuf frames, delta updates, `seq` ranges, compress.)

**What breaks at scale.** A single hot room with 10k concurrent sockets needs presence as an approximate count (not a per-user list), fan-out through a broker, and per-connection backpressure (drop slow consumers, buffer bounded). History deep in the conversation makes pagination expensive (cursor by `seq`, index on `(conversationId, seq)`). WebSocket at the edge needs sticky routing or a broker (Phase 12 â†’ chat/WhatsApp). Attachments must leave the WebSocket (presigned upload, then a reference frame) or you will blow memory limits. And iOS backgrounding will kill sockets: design for resync-on-foreground, not for an eternal connection.

