## 4.6.11.5 Google Docs-style collaborative editor

ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know** (asked as "how would you build X" and as the classic "this is a multi-month project" honesty test)

**Scope honesty first.** State it in your first 30 seconds: *this is a multi-month project; in an interview I will scope it to the hard parts and the trade-offs.* Then design within that scope. Interviewers reward this framing more than a confident fake design.

**Requirements (scoped).** Multi-user text editing with live presence, conflict-free convergence, undo/redo, autosave and version history, comments. **Out of scope for the interview answer:** tables, images, equations, drawing, offline-first sync, printing, plugin API, and mobile editing.

**The core problem: convergence.** Two families of algorithms:

| | OT (Operational Transform) | CRDT |
|---|---|---|
| Idea | Transform each operation against concurrent operations so all replicas converge | Represent data as a conflict-free structure; merge is commutative/idempotent, so order does not matter |
| Central piece | A **transformation function** per operation pair, e.g. `t_i(insert(5, 'A')) â†’ insert(5 + |L|)` | A sequence CRDT (RGA) with unique IDs per character |
| Server | **Central authoritative server** that orders operations and broadcasts transformed ops | Any peer; ops can arrive in any order |
| Rich text | Hard (formatting ops interact with ranges; needs per-attribute transforms) | Easier conceptually (per-attribute LWW with proper merges) |
| Cursor/presence | Position transforms are fiddly (needs offset â†’ position mapping) | Native: positions are CRDT positions |
| Offline | Requires server authority and a reconnect protocol | Natural fit (merge on reconnect) |
| Cost to build | Weeks per operation type, and every new op type needs a transform | Months to get a production-quality sequence CRDT |
| Used by | Google Docs (operational transform family) | Figma, and most modern collaborative editors |

**Interview answer structure:** "I would use a CRDT (RGA-style sequence CRDT with unique character IDs and per-attribute last-writer-wins registers), because presence/cursors come for free and offline merging is tractable, accepting a higher memory cost per document (tombstones) and a larger initial build. I would keep a central server for auth, document membership, persistence, and snapshotting, but it would not need to order operations. If we only ever need online collaboration with a single central authority, OT is cheaper to ship."

**Presence and cursors.** Separate channel from document content (never persist it, never version it, never CRDT it). Send throttled (~30â€“60 Hz, and only on change) relative positions (e.g. `{ leftAnchor: 'char-id-8a3f', offset: 3 }`) rather than absolute offsets â€” a relative position survives concurrent edits; an absolute offset does not. Decay presence (a cursor with no update for 10 s disappears), and send an explicit `leave` on unload so cursors do not litter the document.

**Undo/redo semantics â€” the subtle part.** Naive per-user undo stacks break: undoing your own insert after someone else inserted inside it must not delete their text. Correct approach: record undo entries with **intent ranges** ("the run of text I typed") and undo by deleting the surviving characters within that range, not by replaying inverse operations against current state. With a CRDT this is a range deletion over currently-surviving IDs; with OT it is a transformed inverse. Only undo *your own* actions (collaborative editors do not offer "undo what she did"). Keep the stack bounded (50â€“100 entries) and clear redo on a new local action after an undo.

**Autosave and versioning.** Snapshots, not a save button: server persists updates to an append-only log with a periodic compacted snapshot (e.g. every 100 updates or 10 s of inactivity); version history is created on explicit milestones and on a schedule (keep last N versions plus named versions); every snapshot is independently loadable so recovery does not require replaying the whole log. Compaction requires tombstone GC â€” which is safe only once all clients have acknowledged past that version (track client clocks and only GC beyond the minimum acknowledged cursor). Include document-level metadata (owner, ACL, created) in the CRDT root so membership changes merge consistently.

**Comments and suggestions.** Model them as **anchored ranges referencing stable CRDT IDs** with a separate comment thread entity; a comment survives concurrent edits because its anchor is relative. Suggestion mode = a proposed range with the replacement text and an author; acceptance applies a real document edit (so it goes through the CRDT and converges) rather than mutating the document behind the CRDT's back. This is a genuinely good design point to say out loud: *suggestions are data; acceptance is an operation.*

**Large-document performance.** Virtualise rendering (only the viewport's text nodes; measure with `IntersectionObserver` on block elements); keep the CRDT document out of the DOM (never render a whole document as DOM nodes); avoid re-creating the editor model on every keystroke; batch remote operations (a microtask or rAF drain, and coalesce remote cursor updates); do not re-parse HTML for the whole document on any local change; consider block-level granularity in the model so a paragraph edit does not dirty the whole document. Budget: typing latency â‰¤ 50 ms for a 20k-word document on a mid-tier laptop; remote ops applied within 200 ms of arrival.

**Offline.** With a CRDT you can hold updates in IndexedDB and flush on reconnect â€” but you must handle the hard cases: editing the same region while offline produces a merge that is technically correct and *semantically* ugly (that is the honest limitation), and access revocation while offline (deny on reconnect and roll back). Say both out loud.

**Accessibility (underrated, and a differentiator).** Collaborative editors are hard for screen-reader users because of constant cursor movement. Provide: a keyboard-accessible command palette for formatting (never mouse-only toolbars), an "accessible view" mode that exposes a linearised document, proper roles for the text body (`role="textbox"` with `aria-multiline` on a contenteditable wrapper, or a textarea-backed custom editor for true a11y), announced collaborator events (`aria-live="polite"`: "Alex joined"), and never let remote cursor movements steal focus or scroll the viewport (a classic bug). Contrast remote cursors on selection and don't rely on colour alone (label each collaborator with a name).

**Multiplayer testing.** Deterministic simulation: seed a document, generate N random concurrent operations, apply them in many different orders, and assert convergence (all replicas identical) â€” this is the test that matters and it is cheap to write with a property-based approach. Then test intent-undo semantics, and test the resync path with an injected packet loss.

**Follow-ups to expect.** CRDT vs OT (above). How do you handle a very large document (10 MB)? (Block granularity, snapshotting, lazy loading of unseen blocks.) How do you implement undo (above). How do you handle a user editing while offline (above). How do you scale the fan-out (server holds the doc state, broadcasts deltas; partition by document on a broker; a snapshotter per doc; Phase 12). How would you scope this for a 3-person team in a quarter (shared text only, no rich formatting, presence as coloured text, no comments; ship single-user editing with CRDT data structures from day one so you do not have to migrate).

**What breaks at scale.** Per-document memory server-side (mitigate: snapshot + delta, GC tombstones beyond the acknowledged cursor); CRDT growth from tombstones and metadata (mitigate: compaction with GC, per-user undo depth limits); a hot document with hundreds of concurrent editors (mitigate: partition by document, per-doc lock-free ordering not required by CRDT but snapshot rate must be tuned, and awareness fan-out is a per-doc pub/sub); the awareness channel at 60 Hz Ã— 500 users = 30k messages/second per document (mitigate: throttle per user, send cursors only for users in the viewport â€” "cursor interest management", which is what real editors do).

